                  Access to Hidden Pointers
     Allows You to Code Class Browsers and Other Advanced Tools
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ottawa, Ontario, Canada - October 10, 1992 - Code Farms Inc. has announced a new
utility now available as a part of their C/C++ data library.

If you want to design your own class browsers,  fast persistent data,  or other
advanced C++   tools,   you need information on how the classes are constructed
(meta class information),  including the inheritance graph and locations of all
the virtual function and virtual class pointers.

Unfortunately, different compilers construct classes in different ways, and most
C++ compilers keep this information hidden from the user.

Code Farms discovered a new simple method which can retrieve this information in
in a completely general way,  without mimicking the logic of the compiler.  Code
Farms' persistent data has been based on this idea for over a year. The feature
is now available to all users of the library.

Based on the observation that "standard" class  libraries  are quickly becoming
obsolete,  because they lead to spaghetti++ code on large projects,  Code Farms
developed a new  generation  C++   library,   which fully separates objects and
relations for data structures such as linked lists,  collections, aggregations,
associations, trees, graphs, hash tables, properties, and ER models.

"This  is  a  state-of-the-art  technology  which  cannot  be  implemented with
templates",  says Jiri Soukup,  president of Code Farms.  "We have  a clean and
simple implementation of Booch's mechanism. Programs designed with this library
are easier to debug and maintain,  and require less memory than those  designed
with traditional templates."

The type tables, the inheritance graph, and the hidden pointers are available as
a new library class, called "TYPE". This class includes iterators that traverse
base classes of a given class, and inter-class (relational) pointers.

Code Farms' data objects are automatically persistent(no user-coded IO functions
required),and have superior run-time performance. The library includes fast disk
storage (memory blasting), data browser, and can be used as a database maker.

The software runs with most C++ compilers under UNIX,  DOS,  WINDOWS,  and Mac:
AT&T, SUN, HP, MIPS, Borland, Zortech, and Microsoft. Both programs and data are
portable between  different  environments.   A  similar library is available in
regular C.

The new feature will be demostrated at OOPSLA-92 Conference,  Vancouver, B.C.,
October 19-21, 1992.
